<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patrick John</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Patrick John was a Dominican trade unionist and politician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a> Labour Party who became the island's first prime minister on independence in 1978. His government faced strikes and mass protests, and he was forced from office in 1979. He was later convicted of involvement in a 1981 plot by foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> to restore him to power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Patrick Roland John was born on 7 January 1938. He led the Waterfront and Allied Workers' Union and was mayor of Roseau before entering the House of Assembly for Roseau North on 1 November 1970. He became Deputy Premier and Minister of Finance in 1973 and, after the resignation of Edward Oliver LeBlanc in 1974, Premier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a>. His Labour Party government founded the National Commercial &amp; Development Bank, replaced shanty dwellings in Roseau with housing at Bath Estate and River Estate, opened a deepwater harbour at Fond Cole and started the Dominica Social Security system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Division grew after the murder of a number of tourists in the mid-1970s and the emergence of a black power movement. In 1974 his government passed the Prohibited and Undesirable Societies Act, known as the Dread Act, which made it legal to arrest any Dread without a warrant and to deny bail to anyone wearing dreadlocks. On 3 November 1978 John became the first Prime Minister of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a>; he favoured a mixed economy and ties with the West, and established diplomatic relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>.</p><p>On 29 May 1979 a clash outside parliament left two people dead, among them a young man named Phillip Timothy. Public servants went on strike, the Committee for National Salvation under Charles Savarin called a general strike, and Labour ministers resigned one by one, beginning with Oliver Seraphin. President Frederick Degazon fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> on 11 June, and the House of Assembly voted John out of office on 20 June 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John lost his seat in the 1980 election. In 1981 he was arrested over Operation Red Dog, a plot to restore him to power with Canadian and American citizens largely affiliated with white supremacist and Ku Klux Klan groups. On 23 October 1985 he was found guilty of conspiring to overthrow the government and sentenced to 12 years. Pardoned by Mary Eugenia Charles, he was released on 29 May 1990.</p><p>He then led the Dominica Football Association, which became a FIFA affiliate in 1994, and in 2011 FIFA banned him from the sport for two years over an alleged bribery scheme. He died in Roseau on 6 July 2021, at the age of 83.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Patrick John” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Colony of Dominica became independent on 3 November 1978 as a republic within the Commonwealth, with a president elected by parliament and <b>Patrick John</b> as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/commonwealth-of-dominica">Commonwealth of Dominica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Roseau, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominica">Dominica</a>. Died in Roseau, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominica">Dominica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.3,-61.38&amp;year=1991" aria-label="Roseau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Patrick John"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4886 6243h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4886 6243h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.3,-61.38&amp;year=1991">What was here in 1991? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
